# Why Not Run MMR Separately?

Suppose:

Dense Search:

```text
Embedding chunk 1
Embedding chunk 2
Embedding chunk 3
```

Sparse Search:

```text
Keyword chunk 1
Keyword chunk 2
Keyword chunk 3
```

If MMR runs separately:

```text
Dense MMR → 3 docs
Sparse MMR → 3 docs
```

You may still end up with:

```text
6 overlapping docs
```

because neither side knows what the other selected.

Global diversity is impossible.

---

Production systems usually prefer:

```text
Global MMR
```

over

```text
Per-search MMR
```

---

# Typical Enterprise Pipeline

A very common pipeline:

```text
Query
 ↓
Dense Search
 ↓
Top 50

Query
 ↓
BM25 Search
 ↓
Top 50

      ↓
 Reciprocal Rank Fusion
      ↓
Top 100 Candidates
      ↓
MMR
      ↓
Top 20 Diverse Candidates
      ↓
Cross Encoder Reranker
      ↓
Top 5
      ↓
LLM
```

This is much closer to what you'd see in a production RAG system.

---

# What Happens During Fusion?

Let's say:

Dense results:

| Doc | Rank |
| --- | ---- |
| A   | 1    |
| B   | 2    |
| C   | 3    |

Sparse results:

| Doc | Rank |
| --- | ---- |
| C   | 1    |
| D   | 2    |
| E   | 3    |

After RRF:

```text
A
C
B
D
E
```

Now MMR gets:

```text
[A,C,B,D,E]
```

as candidates.

---

# But MMR Needs Embeddings

This is important.

MMR computes:

```text
Similarity(Query, Document)
```

and

```text
Similarity(Document, OtherDocuments)
```

using embeddings.

So even if BM25 found:

```text
Document X
```

MMR still needs an embedding for Document X.

Fortunately:

```text
All chunks already have embeddings
```

inside the vector database.

So MMR can compute diversity scores.

---

# Real-World Asynchronous Retrieval

You asked another very important production question.

Yes.

Dense and sparse retrieval usually run in parallel.

---

Example:

```text
                 ┌──── Dense Search
User Query ──────┤
                 └──── Sparse Search
```

Both start simultaneously.

---

Using Python:

```python
dense_task = asyncio.create_task(
    dense_retriever(query)
)

sparse_task = asyncio.create_task(
    bm25_retriever(query)
)

dense_results, sparse_results = await asyncio.gather(
    dense_task,
    sparse_task
)
```

This saves latency.

---

Without Parallelism

```text
Dense Search 100 ms
+
Sparse Search 100 ms

=
200 ms
```

---

With Parallelism

```text
max(100,100)

=
100 ms
```

---

This is why virtually every enterprise RAG system runs retrieval concurrently.

---

# When Does MMR Execute?

After both searches finish.

```text
Query
   ↓
Dense Search ──┐
               │
               ├─ Wait
               │
Sparse Search ─┘
       ↓
Fusion
       ↓
MMR
       ↓
Reranker
```

MMR cannot start until it knows the full candidate pool.

---

# What If Dense Finishes Earlier?

Suppose:

```text
Dense = 50 ms
Sparse = 200 ms
```

MMR still waits.

```text
Dense complete
      ↓
Waiting...
      ↓
Sparse complete
      ↓
Fusion
      ↓
MMR
```

because MMR needs the final combined candidate set.

