# Modern Vector Databases

Some systems already integrate hybrid retrieval:

* [Qdrant](https://qdrant.tech?utm_source=chatgpt.com)
* [Weaviate](https://weaviate.io?utm_source=chatgpt.com)
* [OpenSearch](https://opensearch.org?utm_source=chatgpt.com)
* [Elasticsearch](https://www.elastic.co?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Internally they may perform:

```text
Dense Search
+
Sparse Search
+
Fusion
```

inside the database itself.

Then your application receives:

```text
Top 100 hybrid candidates
```

and applies:

```text
MMR
↓
Reranker
```

outside the database.

---

# What Do Large Production Systems Usually Do?

A common architecture looks like:

```text
User Query
    ↓
Query Rewrite
    ↓
────────────────────────
│                      │
Dense Search       BM25 Search
│                      │
────────────────────────
           ↓
      RRF Fusion
           ↓
         MMR
           ↓
 Cross Encoder Reranker
           ↓
 Context Compression
           ↓
           LLM
```

So in most modern production RAG systems:

✅ Dense and sparse retrieval run asynchronously/parallel.

✅ Their outputs are merged (RRF, weighted fusion, etc.).

✅ MMR receives the **combined candidate set**, not just dense results.

✅ MMR then removes redundancy across *all* retrieved candidates before reranking.

This "Fusion → MMR → Reranker" ordering is one of the most common high-quality enterprise retrieval pipelines today.



# question: what is rrf or weigted fusion


These are the two most common techniques used to **combine Dense Search results and Sparse Search results** in Hybrid Retrieval.

The problem they solve is:

```text
Dense Search says:
A, B, C, D

BM25 says:
C, E, F, G
```

How do we create one final ranking?

That's where **RRF (Reciprocal Rank Fusion)** and **Weighted Fusion** come in.

---

# Why Fusion Is Needed

Imagine the query:

> "What is the attention mask in BERT?"

Dense search might return:

| Rank | Doc                   |
| ---- | --------------------- |
| 1    | BERT architecture     |
| 2    | Transformer attention |
| 3    | Positional encoding   |

because semantically they're related.

---

BM25 might return:

| Rank | Doc                         |
| ---- | --------------------------- |
| 1    | Attention mask in BERT      |
| 2    | BERT attention mask example |
| 3    | HuggingFace attention_mask  |

because exact keywords match.

---

Both retrieval methods found useful information.

You don't want to choose one and ignore the other.

You want:

```text
Dense
+
Sparse
=
Better Recall
```

Fusion combines them.

---

# Method 1: RRF (Reciprocal Rank Fusion)

RRF is the most popular method in modern RAG.

Many production systems use it because it is:

* Simple
* Fast
* Robust
* Doesn't require score normalization

---

## Core Idea

Don't use retrieval scores.

Use ranks only.

Example:

### Dense Search

| Rank | Doc |
| ---- | --- |
| 1    | A   |
| 2    | B   |
| 3    | C   |

---

### Sparse Search

| Rank | Doc |
| ---- | --- |
| 1    | C   |
| 2    | D   |
| 3    | E   |

---

RRF score:

[
Score(d)=\sum \frac{1}{k+r(d)}
]

where:

* (r(d)) = rank
* (k) = constant (usually 60)

---

For document C:

Dense rank:

```text
3
```

Sparse rank:

```text
1
```

Score:

[
\frac1{60+3}
+
\frac1{60+1}
]

---

For document A:

Dense rank:

```text
1
```

Sparse rank:

Not present

Score:

[
\frac1{60+1}
]

---

Notice something:

```text
C appears in BOTH searches
```

So C receives score contributions twice.

It rises to the top.

---

Final ranking may become:

```text
C
A
B
D
E
```

---

# Why RRF Became Popular

Dense scores and BM25 scores are completely different.

Example:

Dense similarity:

```text
0.89
0.87
0.84
```

BM25:

```text
17.2
14.5
9.1
```

These numbers are not comparable.

You can't directly add them.

RRF avoids this issue by ignoring scores entirely.

It only uses rank positions.

---

# Visualization

Dense:

```text
A
B
C
```

Sparse:

```text
C
D
E
```

RRF says:

```text
C appears twice
⇒ Important

A appears once
⇒ Less important

D appears once
⇒ Less important
```

---

# Why Production Systems Love RRF

No tuning required.

Works across:

```text
BM25
Dense Search
Graph Search
Metadata Search
Keyword Search
```

You can fuse anything.

---

# Method 2: Weighted Fusion

Weighted Fusion uses actual scores.

Formula:

[
Final =
w_1(DenseScore)
+
w_2(BM25Score)
]

---

Example

Suppose:

### Dense

| Doc | Score |
| --- | ----- |
| A   | 0.95  |
| B   | 0.90  |
| C   | 0.85  |

---

### BM25

| Doc | Score |
| --- | ----- |
| A   | 15    |
| B   | 5     |
| C   | 1     |

---

First normalize scores.

Dense:

| Doc | Score |
| --- | ----- |
| A   | 0.95  |
| B   | 0.90  |
| C   | 0.85  |

---

BM25 normalized:

| Doc | Score |
| --- | ----- |
| A   | 1.0   |
| B   | 0.33  |
| C   | 0.07  |

---

Then:

[
0.7(Dense)
+
0.3(BM25)
]

---

For A:

[
0.7(0.95)
+
0.3(1.0)
========

0.965
]

---

For B:

[
0.7(0.90)
+
0.3(0.33)
=========

0.729
]

---

Now rank by final score.

---

# Why Weighted Fusion Is Hard

The challenge:

```text
Dense scores
and
BM25 scores

live on different scales
```

Example:

Dense:

```text
0.91
0.89
0.87
```

BM25:

```text
35
18
7
```

Need normalization.

Need tuning.

Need experimentation.

Can break when datasets change.

---

# RRF vs Weighted Fusion

| Feature             | RRF | Weighted Fusion |
| ------------------- | --- | --------------- |
| Uses ranks          | ✅   | ❌               |
| Uses scores         | ❌   | ✅               |
| Needs normalization | ❌   | ✅               |
| Easy to implement   | ✅   | ❌               |
| Robust              | ✅   | ⚠️              |
| Tunable             | ❌   | ✅               |
| Most common in RAG  | ✅   | ⚠️              |
| Production friendly | ✅   | ✅               |

---

